WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.980
If we are to write a sophisticated light simulation
program, and we write a list of features that

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:15.230
we really wish to have, we should definitely
keep an eye on defocus effects.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.890
This is what it looks like, and in order to
do that, our simulation program has to take

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:26.119
into consideration the geometry and thickness
of the lenses within our virtual camera, and

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:31.689
even though it looks absolutely amazing, it
is very costly to simulate that properly.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:37.180
This particular technique attempts to do this
in real time, and, for specialized display

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:44.000
types, typically ones that are found in head-mounted
displays for Virtual Reality applications.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.080
So here we go, due to popular request, a little
VR in Two Minute Papers.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.940
In virtual reality, defocus effects are especially
important because they mimic how the human

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.000
visual system works.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.860
Only a tiny region that we’re focusing on
looks sharp, and everything else should be

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.070
blurry, but not any kind of blurry, it has
to look physically plausible.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:12.250
If we can pull this off just right, we’ll
get a great and immersive VR experience.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:17.790
The heart of this problem is looking at a
2D image and being able to estimate how far

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.450
away different objects are from the camera
lens.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.630
This is a task that is relatively easy for
humans because we have an intuitive understanding

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:32.020
of depth and geometry, but of course, this
is no easy task for a machine.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:36.909
To accomplish this, here, a convolutional
neural network is used, and our seasoned Fellow

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:42.010
Scholars know that this means that we need
a ton of training data.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:46.310
The input should be a bunch of images, and
their corresponding depth maps for the neural

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.890
network to learn from.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.780
The authors implemented this with a random
scene generator, which creates a bunch of

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:58.440
these crazy scenes with a lot of occlusions
and computes via simulation the appropriate

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.470
depth map for them.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:04.350
On the right, you see these depth maps, or
in other words, images that describe to the

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.540
computer how far away these objects are.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:13.180
The incredible thing is that the neural network
was able to learn the concept of occlusions,

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.340
and was able to create super high quality
defocus effects.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:23.040
Not only that, but this technique can also
be reconfigured to fit different use cases:

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.340
if we are okay with spending up to 50 milliseconds
to render an image, which is 20 frames per

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:34.799
second, we can get super high-quality images,
or, if we only have a budget of 5 milliseconds

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:40.019
per image, which is 200 frames per second,
we can do that and the quality of the outputs

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:42.609
degrades just a tiny bit.

00:02:42.609 --> 00:02:46.969
While we are talking about image quality,
let’s have a closer look at the paper, where

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:52.590
we see a ton of comparisons against previous
works and of course, against the baseline

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.349
ground truth knowledge.

00:02:54.349 --> 00:03:00.969
You see two metrics here: PSNR, which is the
peak signal to noise ratio, and SSIM, the

00:03:00.969 --> 00:03:03.150
structural similarity metric.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.120
In this case, both are used to measure how
close the output of these techniques is to

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.090
the ground truth footage.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.579
Both are subject to maximization.

00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:16.680
For instance, here you see that the second
best technique has a peak signal to noise

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.120
ratio of around 40, and this new method scores
45.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.540
Well, some may think that that’s just a
12-ish percent difference, right?

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.540
… No.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.900
Note that PSNR works on a logarithmic scale,
which means that even a tiny difference in

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.029
numbers translates to a huge difference in
terms of visuals.

00:03:37.029 --> 00:03:42.269
You can see in the closeups that the output
of this new method is close to indistinguishable

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:43.370
from the ground truth.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:49.230
A neural network that successfully learned
the concept of occlusions and depth by looking

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.969
at random scenes.

00:03:50.969 --> 00:03:51.969
Bravo.

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:56.689
As virtual reality applications are on the
rise these days, this technique will be useful

00:03:56.689 --> 00:04:00.150
to provide a more immersive experience for
the users.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.739
And to make sure that this method sees more
widespread use, the authors also made the

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:09.900
source code and the training datasets available
for everyone, free of charge, so make sure

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.650
to have a look at that and run your own experiments
if you’re interested.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.459
I'll be doing that in the meantime.

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:18.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

